      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "μὴ βεβιωκὼς εὐσεβῶς",
          "english_quote": "if I have not lived devoutly",
          "issue": "The conditional rendering of this circumstantial perfect participle is defensible, but the notes do not disclose the materially different causal reading, “because I have not lived devoutly,” or the less resolved “not having lived devoutly.” The choice affects whether deficient conduct is presented as a condition or as an assumed circumstance within his feared scenario.",
          "suggested_revision": "Either preserve the participial wording as “not having lived devoutly,” or retain the conditional main rendering and add a note explaining that the surrounding hypothetical fear supports it while preserving the causal alternative. This ordinary attachment ambiguity does not require source verification or a blocked verdict.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "οὔτε τίς λόγος βέβαιος εὑρίσκεται",
          "english_quote": "nor is any certain argument to be found",
          "issue": "The supplied τίς is interrogative. In parallel with the preceding εἰ-clause, this clause is defensibly understood as another indirect question governed by γινώσκω: he does not know what certain argument is to be found. The draft instead asserts that no certain argument is available, moving the negation from his knowledge to the existence or availability of an argument. Its notes do not justify or disclose that change.",
          "suggested_revision": "Replace the clause with “nor do I know what certain argument is to be found.” This preserves the printed interrogative and the epistemic limitation without asserting that no reliable argument exists. Submit the replacement for fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    }
